<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Croatian–Hungarian Settlement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Nagodba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Croatian–Hungarian Settlement, or Nagodba, was the agreement of 1868 that defined the place of Croatia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Austro-Hungarian Compromise of 1867</a>. Croatia-Slavonia kept its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-parliament">Sabor</a> and control of internal affairs, justice, education and religion, under a ban appointed on the Hungarian prime minister's advice, while finance, defence and other common affairs were handled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/croatian-hungarian-settlement.jpg" alt="Croatian–Hungarian Settlement" width="320" height="351" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roberta. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rijecka_krpica_HDA_300109.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848</a> the northern Croatian lands formed the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>, separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> crown lands under the jurisdiction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> that functioned in practice as a single kingdom, subordinate to the government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>; imperial officials called it the Kingdom of Croatia-Slavonia. The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> in the south, taken from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> in 1815, stayed a separate division of the Austrian half of the monarchy.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Austro-Hungarian Compromise of 1867</a> placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> under Hungarian jurisdiction, Levin Rauch replaced Josip Šokčević as acting ban. A new electoral law imposed by the king on 20 October 1867 cut the elected members to 66 and added unelected virilist members. In the election held from 19 November to 23 December 1867 the People's Party won only 14 seats, and Rauch's Unionist Party gained a majority in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-parliament">Sabor</a>, which accepted the Nagodba on 24 September 1868, though many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> who wanted full autonomy for the South Slavs objected.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The settlement was a joint enactment of the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, sanctioned by the Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> as King of Hungary, and so became a fundamental law of both. The Croatian parliament elected 29 deputies to the House of Representatives of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-diet">Hungarian Diet</a> and two members to its House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">Magnates</a>. The common Diet controlled the military, finance, commercial and mining law, customs, the post, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraphs</a>, railways, harbours and shipping. Article 66 listed the lands of the kingdom, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, although Dalmatia remained in the Austrian half.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The kingdom was now officially named the Kingdom of Croatia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>. Under Article 66 the city and port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Fiume</a>, now Rijeka, became a corpus separatum attached to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, a question the settlement left unresolved, for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> regarded the port as its own. In 1881 the Croatian Military Frontier and the Slavonian Military Frontier were incorporated into the kingdom, and its deputies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-diet">Hungarian Diet</a> rose to 40 and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> to three. The settlement lasted until 29 October 1918, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-parliament">Croatian Parliament</a> ended all state ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Croatian–Hungarian Settlement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Croatian-Hungarian Settlement</b> of 1868 joined the kingdom with Slavonia as the Kingdom of Croatia-Slavonia, an autonomous part of the Hungarian half of Austria-Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-habsburg">Kingdom of Croatia (Habsburg)</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Croatian-Hungarian Settlement</b> of 1868, known as the Nagodba, joined the kingdoms of Croatia and Slavonia into one autonomous unit within the Lands of the Hungarian Crown.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-slavonia">Kingdom of Croatia-Slavonia</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Croatian-Hungarian Settlement</b> of 1868 gave Croatia-Slavonia autonomy within it.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hungary-1867-1918">Kingdom of Hungary (1867-1918)</a></li><li><blockquote>Slavonia remained formally a separate kingdom within the Lands of the Hungarian Crown until the <b>Croatian-Hungarian Settlement</b> of 1868 joined it with Croatia as the Kingdom of Croatia-Slavonia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-slavonia">Kingdom of Slavonia</a></li></ul></section>
